# API Controllers

Controllers are **thin HTTP adapters**. They bind a `Request`, call its `FromRequest()`, invoke an `Action`, then map the result to a `Resource`.

## Rules

- Decorate with `[ApiController]`, `[Route("api/...")]`, `[Authorize]`.
- Protect every endpoint with `[HasPermission(RequestClass.Permission)]` — read the permission string from the Request class constant, never hardcode it in the controller.
- Inject domain Actions via constructor; no business logic, no `DbContext`, no repositories here.
- For writes return `Ok(new { message = "feature.action_succeeded" })` with a dotted i18n key.
- For reads return `Ok(ResourceType.FromDto(await _action.ExecuteAsync(...)))`.
- For list endpoints use `.ToResourceCollection<TDto, TResource>(TResource.FromDto)`.
- Throw via Shared exceptions (`NotFoundException`, `ValidationException`, ...) from the Action — don't try/catch in the controller.

## Example (matches the real `InvoicesController`)

```csharp
[ApiController]
[Route("api/admin/invoices")]
[Authorize]
public class InvoicesController(CreateInvoiceAction createInvoiceAction) : ControllerBase
{
    [HttpPost]
    [HasPermission(CreateInvoiceRequest.Permission)]
    public async Task<ActionResult> Create([FromBody] CreateInvoiceRequest request)
    {
        await createInvoiceAction.ExecuteAsync(request.FromRequest());
        return Ok(new { message = "invoices.created_successfully" });
    }

    [HttpGet("{id:long}")]
    [HasPermission(GetInvoiceRequest.Permission)]
    public async Task<ActionResult<InvoiceResource>> Get(long id) =>
        Ok(InvoiceResource.FromDto(
            await _getInvoiceAction.ExecuteAsync(new GetInvoiceRequest { Id = id }.FromRequest())));
}
```

## Anti-patterns

- ❌ Business logic, EF queries, or `if`-chains inside the controller.
- ❌ Hardcoded permission strings — use `RequestClass.Permission`.
- ❌ Returning raw entities or anonymous shapes for success data — always use a `*Resource`.
- ❌ Catching domain exceptions and rewrapping them.
